#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: description
#, no-wrap
msgid "A free IBM course on quantum information and computation"
msgstr "Isang libreng IBM course sa quantum information at computation"

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#, no-wrap
msgid "The 9-qubit Shor code"
msgstr "Ang 9-qubit na Shor code"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now we turn to the 9-qubit Shor code, which is a quantum error correcting code obtained by combining together the two codes considered in the previous section: the 3-bit repetition code for qubits, which allows for the correction of a single bit-flip error, and the modified version of that code, which allows for the correction of a single phase-flip error.\n"
msgstr "Ngayon ay lilipat tayo sa 9-qubit na Shor code, na isang quantum error correcting code na nakuha sa pamamagitan ng pagsasama ng dalawang code na tinalakay sa nakaraang seksyon: ang 3-bit repetition code para sa mga qubit, na nagpapahintulot ng pagwawasto ng iisang bit-flip error, at ang binagong bersyon ng code na iyon, na nagpapahintulot ng pagwawasto ng iisang phase-flip error.\n"

#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Code description {#code-description}"
msgstr "Paglalarawan ng code {#code-description}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The 9-qubit Shor code is the code we obtain by *concatenating* the two codes from the previous section.\n"
"This means that we first apply one encoding, which encodes one qubit into three, and then we apply the other encoding to *each* of the three qubits used for the first encoding, resulting in nine qubits in total.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang 9-qubit na Shor code ang makukuha natin sa pamamagitan ng *concatenation* ng dalawang code mula sa nakaraang seksyon.\n"
"Ibig sabihin, unang mag-a-apply tayo ng isang encoding, na nag-encode ng isang qubit sa tatlo, at pagkatapos ay ia-apply natin ang isa pang encoding sa *bawat isa* sa tatlong qubit na ginamit para sa unang encoding, na nagreresulta sa siyam na qubit sa kabuuan.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To be more precise, while we could apply the two codes in either order in this particular case, we'll make the choice to first apply the modified version of the 3-bit repetition code (which detects phase-flip errors), and then we'll encode *each* of the resulting three qubits independently using the original 3-bit repetition code (which detects bit-flip errors).\n"
"Here is a circuit diagram representation of this encoding.\n"
msgstr ""
"Para maging mas tumpak, kahit na maaari tayong mag-apply ng dalawang code sa alinmang pagkakasunod-sunod sa partikular na kasong ito, pipiliin nating i-apply muna ang binagong bersyon ng 3-bit repetition code (na nakaka-detect ng mga phase-flip error), at pagkatapos ay ie-encode natin ang *bawat isa* sa tatlong qubit na resulta nito nang hiwalay gamit ang orihinal na 3-bit repetition code (na nakaka-detect ng mga bit-flip error).\n"
"Narito ang isang circuit diagram na representasyon ng encoding na ito.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Encoding circuit for the 9-qubit Shor code showing three blocks](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/correcting-quantum-errors/Shor-encoder.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Encoding circuit para sa 9-qubit na Shor code na nagpapakita ng tatlong bloke](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/correcting-quantum-errors/Shor-encoder.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"As the figure suggests, we'll think about the nine qubits of the Shor code as being grouped into three blocks of three qubits, where each block is obtained from the *second* encoding step (which is the ordinary 3-bit repetition code).\n"
"The ordinary 3-bit repetition code, which here is applied three times independently, is called the *inner code* in this context, whereas the *outer code* is the code used for the first encoding step, which is the modified version of the 3-bit repetition code that detects phase-flip errors.\n"
msgstr ""
"Tulad ng iminumungkahi ng figure, iisipin natin ang siyam na qubit ng Shor code bilang naka-grupo sa tatlong bloke ng tatlong qubit, kung saan ang bawat bloke ay nakuha mula sa *pangalawang* encoding step (na siyang ordinaryong 3-bit repetition code).\n"
"Ang ordinaryong 3-bit repetition code, na dito ay ina-apply nang tatlong beses nang hiwalay, ay tinatawag na *inner code* sa kontekstong ito, samantalang ang *outer code* naman ang code na ginamit para sa unang encoding step, na siyang binagong bersyon ng 3-bit repetition code na nakaka-detect ng mga phase-flip error.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We can alternatively specify the code by describing how the two standard basis states for our original qubit get encoded.\n"
msgstr "Maaari rin nating tukuyin ang code sa pamamagitan ng paglalarawan kung paano nako-encode ang dalawang standard basis state ng ating orihinal na qubit.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\vert 0\\rangle &\n"
"\\:\\mapsto\\:\n"
"\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\n"
"(\\vert 000\\rangle + \\vert 111\\rangle) \\otimes\n"
"(\\vert 000\\rangle + \\vert 111\\rangle) \\otimes\n"
"(\\vert 000\\rangle + \\vert 111\\rangle) \\\\[4mm]\n"
"\\vert 1\\rangle &\n"
"\\:\\mapsto\\:\n"
"\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\n"
"(\\vert 000\\rangle - \\vert 111\\rangle) \\otimes\n"
"(\\vert 000\\rangle - \\vert 111\\rangle) \\otimes\n"
"(\\vert 000\\rangle - \\vert 111\\rangle)\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"\\begin{aligned}\n"
"\\vert 0\\rangle &\n"
"\\:\\mapsto\\:\n"
"\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\n"
"(\\vert 000\\rangle + \\vert 111\\rangle) \\otimes\n"
"(\\vert 000\\rangle + \\vert 111\\rangle) \\otimes\n"
"(\\vert 000\\rangle + \\vert 111\\rangle) \\\\[4mm]\n"
"\\vert 1\\rangle &\n"
"\\:\\mapsto\\:\n"
"\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\n"
"(\\vert 000\\rangle - \\vert 111\\rangle) \\otimes\n"
"(\\vert 000\\rangle - \\vert 111\\rangle) \\otimes\n"
"(\\vert 000\\rangle - \\vert 111\\rangle)\n"
"\\end{aligned}\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Once we know this, we can determine by linearity how an arbitrary qubit state vector is encoded.\n"
msgstr "Kapag nalaman na natin ito, maaari nating matukoy sa pamamagitan ng linearity kung paano nako-encode ang isang arbitrary na qubit state vector.\n"

#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Correcting bit-flip and phase-flip errors {#correcting-bit-flip-and-phase-flip-errors}"
msgstr "Pagwawasto ng mga bit-flip at phase-flip error {#correcting-bit-flip-and-phase-flip-errors}"

#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Errors and CNOT gates {#errors-and-cnot-gates}"
msgstr "Mga error at CNOT gates {#errors-and-cnot-gates}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To analyze how $X$ and $Z$ errors affect encodings of qubits, both for the 9-qubit Shor code as well as other codes, it will be helpful to observe a few simple relationships between these errors and CNOT gates.\n"
"As we begin to analyze the 9-qubit Shor code, this is a reasonable moment to pause to do this.\n"
msgstr ""
"Para masuri kung paano nakakaapekto ang mga $X$ at $Z$ error sa mga encoding ng mga qubit, kapwa para sa 9-qubit na Shor code at sa ibang mga code, magiging kapaki-pakinabang na obserbahan ang ilang simpleng relasyon sa pagitan ng mga error na ito at mga CNOT gate.\n"
"Habang sisimulan nating suriin ang 9-qubit na Shor code, ito ay isang magandang pagkakataon na huminto at gawin ito.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The following circuit diagrams illustrate three basic relationships among $X$ gates and CNOT gates.\n"
"Specifically, applying an $X$ gate to the *target* qubit prior to a CNOT is equivalent to swapping the order and performing the CNOT first, but applying an $X$ gate to the *control* qubit prior to a CNOT is equivalent to applying $X$ gates to both qubits after the CNOT.\n"
"Finally, applying $X$ gates to both qubits prior to a CNOT is equivalent to applying the CNOT first and then applying an $X$ gate to the control qubit.\n"
"These relationships can be verified by performing the required matrix multiplications or computing the effect of the circuits on standard basis states.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang mga sumusunod na circuit diagram ay naglalarawan ng tatlong pangunahing relasyon sa pagitan ng mga $X$ gate at mga CNOT gate.\n"
"Sa partikular, ang pag-apply ng isang $X$ gate sa *target* qubit bago ang isang CNOT ay katumbas ng pagpapalit ng pagkakasunod at pagsasagawa muna ng CNOT, ngunit ang pag-apply ng isang $X$ gate sa *control* qubit bago ang isang CNOT ay katumbas ng pag-apply ng mga $X$ gate sa parehong qubit pagkatapos ng CNOT.\n"
"Sa wakas, ang pag-apply ng mga $X$ gate sa parehong qubit bago ang isang CNOT ay katumbas ng pagsasagawa muna ng CNOT at pagkatapos ay pag-apply ng isang $X$ gate sa control qubit.\n"
"Mabe-verify ang mga relasyong ito sa pamamagitan ng pagsasagawa ng mga kinakailangang matrix multiplication o sa pag-compute ng epekto ng mga circuit sa mga standard basis state.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![X errors before and after CNOT gates](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/correcting-quantum-errors/X-and-CNOT.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Mga X error bago at pagkatapos ng mga CNOT gate](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/correcting-quantum-errors/X-and-CNOT.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The situation is similar for $Z$ gates, except that the roles of the control and target qubits switch.\n"
"In particular, we have the three relationships depicted by the following quantum circuits.\n"
msgstr ""
"Katulad ang sitwasyon para sa mga $Z$ gate, maliban na ang mga papel ng control at target na qubit ay magpapalit.\n"
"Sa partikular, mayroon tayong tatlong relasyon na inilalarawan ng mga sumusunod na quantum circuit.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Z errors before and after CNOT gates](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/correcting-quantum-errors/Z-and-CNOT.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Mga Z error bago at pagkatapos ng mga CNOT gate](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/correcting-quantum-errors/Z-and-CNOT.svg)\n"

#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Correcting bit-flip errors {#correcting-bit-flip-errors}"
msgstr "Pagwawasto ng mga bit-flip error {#correcting-bit-flip-errors}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now we'll consider how errors can be detected and corrected using the 9-qubit Shor code, starting with bit-flip errors — which we'll generally refer to as $X$ errors hereafter for the sake of brevity.\n"
msgstr "Ngayon ay isasaalang-alang natin kung paano maaaring ma-detect at maitama ang mga error gamit ang 9-qubit na Shor code, simula sa mga bit-flip error — na sa ngayon ay tatawaging $X$ error para sa ikli.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To detect and correct $X$ errors, we can simply treat each of the three blocks in the encoding separately.\n"
"Each block is an encoding of a qubit using the 3-bit repetition code, which protects against $X$ errors — so by performing the syndrome measurements and $X$ error corrections described previously to each block, we can detect and correct up to one $X$ error per block.\n"
"In particular, if there is at most one $X$ error on the nine qubits of the encoding, this error will be detected and corrected by this procedure.\n"
msgstr ""
"Para ma-detect at maitama ang mga $X$ error, maaari tayong tratuhin ang bawat isa sa tatlong bloke sa encoding nang hiwalay.\n"
"Ang bawat bloke ay isang encoding ng isang qubit gamit ang 3-bit repetition code, na nagpoprotekta laban sa mga $X$ error — kaya sa pamamagitan ng pagsasagawa ng mga syndrome measurement at $X$ error correction na inilarawan dati sa bawat bloke, maaari tayong mag-detect at magtama ng hanggang isang $X$ error bawat bloke.\n"
"Sa partikular, kung may hanggang isang $X$ error lamang sa siyam na qubit ng encoding, ang error na ito ay dide-detect at itatama ng pamamaraang ito.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In short, correcting bit-flip errors is a simple matter for this code, due to the fact that the inner code corrects bit-flip errors.\n"
msgstr "Sa madaling salita, ang pagwawasto ng mga bit-flip error ay simpleng bagay para sa code na ito, dahil sa katotohanang itinatama ng inner code ang mga bit-flip error.\n"

#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Correcting phase-flip errors {#correcting-phase-flip-errors}"
msgstr "Pagwawasto ng mga phase-flip error {#correcting-phase-flip-errors}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Next we'll consider phase-flip errors, or $Z$ errors for brevity.\n"
"This time it's not quite as clear what we should do because the outer code is the one that detects $Z$ errors, but the inner code seems to be somehow \"in the way,\" making the detection and correction of these errors slightly more difficult.\n"
msgstr ""
"Susunod ay isasaalang-alang natin ang mga phase-flip error, o $Z$ error para sa ikli.\n"
"Sa pagkakataong ito ay hindi gaanong malinaw kung ano ang dapat nating gawin dahil ang outer code ang nakaka-detect ng mga $Z$ error, ngunit ang inner code ay tila \"nasa daan,\" na ginagawang bahagyang mas mahirap ang pag-detect at pagwawasto ng mga error na ito.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Suppose that a $Z$ error occurs on one of the 9 qubits of the Shor code, such as the one indicated in this diagram.\n"
msgstr "Ipagpalagay na may $Z$ error na nangyayari sa isa sa 9 na qubit ng Shor code, tulad ng ipinapakita sa diagram na ito.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Z error on a qubit for the 9-qubit Shor code](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/correcting-quantum-errors/Shor-phase-error-1.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Z error sa isang qubit para sa 9-qubit na Shor code](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/correcting-quantum-errors/Shor-phase-error-1.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We've already observed what happens when a $Z$ error occurs when we're using the 3-bit repetition code —\n"
"it's equivalent to a $Z$ error occurring prior to encoding.\n"
"In the context of the 9-qubit Shor code, this means that a $Z$ error on any one of the three qubits within a block always has the same effect, which is equivalent to a $Z$ error occurring on the corresponding qubit prior to the inner code being applied.\n"
msgstr ""
"Naobserbahan na natin ang nangyayari kapag may $Z$ error na nagaganap habang ginagamit ang 3-bit repetition code —\n"
"katumbas ito ng isang $Z$ error na nagaganap bago ang encoding.\n"
"Sa konteksto ng 9-qubit na Shor code, ibig sabihin nito ay ang isang $Z$ error sa alinman sa tatlong qubit sa loob ng isang bloke ay palaging may parehong epekto, na katumbas ng isang $Z$ error na nagaganap sa kaukulang qubit bago i-apply ang inner code.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For example, the circuit diagram above is equivalent to the following diagram.\n"
"This can be reasoned using the relationships between $Z$ and CNOT gates described above, or by simply evaluating the circuits on an arbitrary qubit state $\\vert\\psi\\rangle$.\n"
msgstr ""
"Halimbawa, ang circuit diagram sa itaas ay katumbas ng sumusunod na diagram.\n"
"Maaari itong mapatunayan gamit ang mga relasyon sa pagitan ng $Z$ at mga CNOT gate na inilarawan sa itaas, o sa simpleng pag-evaluate ng mga circuit sa isang arbitrary na qubit state $\\vert\\psi\\rangle$.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Z error prior to the inner code for the 9-qubit Shor code](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/correcting-quantum-errors/Shor-phase-error-2.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Z error bago ang inner code para sa 9-qubit na Shor code](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/correcting-quantum-errors/Shor-phase-error-2.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This suggests one option for detecting and correcting $Z$ errors, which is to *decode* the inner code, leaving us with the three qubits used for the outer encoding along with six initialized workspace qubits.\n"
"We can then check these three qubits of the outer code for $Z$ errors, and then finally we can re-encode using the inner code, to bring us back to the 9-qubit encoding we get from the Shor code.\n"
"If we do detect a $Z$ error, we can either correct it prior to re-encoding with the inner code, or we can correct it after re-encoding, by applying a $Z$ gate to any of the qubits in that block.\n"
msgstr ""
"Nagmumungkahi ito ng isang opsyon para sa pag-detect at pagwawasto ng mga $Z$ error, na ang *i-decode* ang inner code, na nag-iiwan sa atin ng tatlong qubit na ginamit para sa outer encoding kasama ang anim na initialized na workspace qubit.\n"
"Maaari nating suriin ang tatlong qubit na ito ng outer code para sa mga $Z$ error, at pagkatapos ay maaari tayong muling mag-encode gamit ang inner code, upang makabalik tayo sa 9-qubit encoding na makukuha natin mula sa Shor code.\n"
"Kung ma-detect natin ang isang $Z$ error, maaari nating itama ito bago muling i-encode gamit ang inner code, o maaari nating itama ito pagkatapos ng muling pag-encode, sa pamamagitan ng pag-apply ng $Z$ gate sa alinman sa mga qubit ng bloke na iyon.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Here's a circuit diagram that includes the encoding circuit and the error suggested above together with the steps just described (but not the actual correction step).\n"
msgstr "Narito ang isang circuit diagram na may kasamang encoding circuit at ang error na iminumungkahi sa itaas kasama ang mga hakbang na inilarawan lamang (ngunit hindi ang aktwal na correction step).\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Z error detection for the 9-qubit Shor code](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/correcting-quantum-errors/Shor-phase-error-detect-1.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Z error detection para sa 9-qubit na Shor code](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/correcting-quantum-errors/Shor-phase-error-detect-1.svg)\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this particular example, the syndrome measurement is $11$, which locates the $Z$ error as having occurred on one of the qubits in the middle block.\n"
msgstr "Sa partikular na halimbawang ito, ang syndrome measurement ay $11$, na nagpapakita na ang $Z$ error ay nangyari sa isa sa mga qubit ng gitnang bloke.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"One advantage of correcting $Z$ errors after the re-encoding step rather than before is that we can simplify the circuit above.\n"
"The following circuit is equivalent, but requires four fewer CNOT gates.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang isang bentahe ng pagwawasto ng mga $Z$ error pagkatapos ng muling pag-encode kaysa bago nito ay maaari nating pasimplehin ang circuit sa itaas.\n"
"Ang sumusunod na circuit ay katumbas, ngunit nangangailangan ng apat na mas kaunting CNOT gate.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Simplified Z error detection for the 9-qubit Shor code](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/correcting-quantum-errors/Shor-phase-error-detect-2.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Pinasimpleng Z error detection para sa 9-qubit na Shor code](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/correcting-quantum-errors/Shor-phase-error-detect-2.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Once again, the syndrome doesn't indicate which qubit has been affected by a $Z$ error, but rather which block has experienced a $Z$ error, with the effect being the same regardless of which qubit within the block was affected.\n"
"We can then correct the error by applying a $Z$ gate to any of the three qubits of whichever block was affected.\n"
msgstr ""
"Muli, ang syndrome ay hindi nagpapahiwatig kung aling qubit ang naapektuhan ng $Z$ error, kundi kung aling bloke ang nakaranas ng $Z$ error, na may parehong epekto anuman sa loob ng bloke ang naapektuhan.\n"
"Maaari nating itama ang error sa pamamagitan ng pag-apply ng $Z$ gate sa alinman sa tatlong qubit ng bloke na naapektuhan.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "As an aside, here we see an example of *degeneracy* in a quantum error-correcting code, where we're able to correct certain errors ($Z$ errors in this case) without being able to identify them uniquely.\n"
msgstr "Bilang panandaliang paglihis, dito ay nakikita natin ang isang halimbawa ng *degeneracy* sa isang quantum error-correcting code, kung saan nagagawa nating iwasto ang ilang partikular na error (mga $Z$ error sa kasong ito) nang hindi natin naitutukoy ang mga ito nang natatangi.\n"

#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Simultaneous bit- and phase-flip errors {#simultaneous-bit-and-phase-flip-errors}"
msgstr "Sabay-sabay na mga bit- at phase-flip error {#simultaneous-bit-and-phase-flip-errors}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We've now seen how both $X$ and $Z$ errors can be detected and corrected using the 9-qubit Shor code, and in particular how at most one $X$ error or at most one $Z$ error can be detected and corrected.\n"
"Now let's suppose that both a bit-flip and a phase-flip error occur, possibly on the same qubit.\n"
"As it turns out, nothing different needs to be done in this situation from what has already been discussed —\n"
"the code is able to detect and correct up to one $X$ error and one $Z$ error simultaneously, without further modification.\n"
msgstr ""
"Nakita na natin ngayon kung paano maaaring ma-detect at maitama ang parehong $X$ at $Z$ error gamit ang 9-qubit na Shor code, at sa partikular kung paano maaaring ma-detect at maitama ang hanggang isang $X$ error o hanggang isang $Z$ error.\n"
"Ngayon ay ipagpalagay nating nagaganap ang parehong isang bit-flip at isang phase-flip error, posibleng sa parehong qubit.\n"
"Gaya ng lumabas, wala nang iba pang kailangang gawin sa sitwasyong ito mula sa mga naipakita na —\n"
"nagagawa ng code na ma-detect at maitama ang hanggang isang $X$ error at isang $Z$ error nang sabay-sabay, nang walang karagdagang pagbabago.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To be more specific, $X$ errors are detected by applying the ordinary 3-bit repetition code syndrome measurement, which is performed separately on each of the three blocks of three qubits; and $Z$ errors are detected through the procedure described just above, which is equivalent to decoding the inner code, performing the syndrome measurement for the modified 3-bit repetition code for phase-flips, and then re-encoding.\n"
"These two error detection steps — as well as the corresponding corrections — can be performed completely independently of one another, and in fact it doesn't matter in which order they're performed.\n"
msgstr ""
"Para maging mas tumpak, ang mga $X$ error ay dide-detect sa pamamagitan ng pag-apply ng ordinaryong 3-bit repetition code syndrome measurement, na isasagawa nang hiwalay sa bawat isa sa tatlong bloke ng tatlong qubit; at ang mga $Z$ error ay dide-detect sa pamamagitan ng pamamaraang inilarawan lamang sa itaas, na katumbas ng pag-decode ng inner code, pagsasagawa ng syndrome measurement para sa binagong 3-bit repetition code para sa mga phase-flip, at pagkatapos ay muling pag-encode.\n"
"Ang dalawang hakbang sa pag-detect ng error na ito — pati na rin ang mga kaukulang pagwawasto — ay maaaring isagawa nang ganap na hiwalay sa isa't isa, at sa katunayan ay hindi mahalaga kung saang pagkakasunod-sunod sila isasagawa.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "To see why this is, consider the example depicted in the following circuit diagram, where both an $X$ and a $Z$ error have affected the bottom qubit of the middle block.\n"
msgstr "Para makita kung bakit ganito, isaalang-alang ang halimbawang inilalarawan sa sumusunod na circuit diagram, kung saan parehong nakaapekto ang $X$ at $Z$ error sa pinakaibabang qubit ng gitnang bloke.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![An XZ error for the 9-qubit Shor code](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/correcting-quantum-errors/Shor-XZ-error.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Isang XZ error para sa 9-qubit na Shor code](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/correcting-quantum-errors/Shor-XZ-error.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Let's first observe that the ordering of the errors doesn't matter, in the sense that reversing the position of the $X$ and $Z$ errors yields an equivalent circuit.\n"
"To be clear, $X$ and $Z$ do not commute, they *anti-commute*:\n"
msgstr ""
"Unang obserbahan natin na ang pagkakasunod-sunod ng mga error ay hindi mahalaga, sa kahulugang ang pagpapalit ng posisyon ng $X$ at $Z$ error ay nagbibigay ng katumbas na circuit.\n"
"Para maging malinaw, ang $X$ at $Z$ ay hindi nagko-commute, sila ay *nag-a-anti-commute*:\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"XZ =\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 1\\\\[1mm]\n"
"1 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"1 & 0\\\\[1mm]\n"
"0 & -1\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & -1\\\\[1mm]\n"
"1 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= - \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"1 & 0\\\\[1mm]\n"
"0 & -1\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 1\\\\[1mm]\n"
"1 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= -ZX.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"XZ =\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 1\\\\[1mm]\n"
"1 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"1 & 0\\\\[1mm]\n"
"0 & -1\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & -1\\\\[1mm]\n"
"1 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= - \\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"1 & 0\\\\[1mm]\n"
"0 & -1\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"\\begin{pmatrix}\n"
"0 & 1\\\\[1mm]\n"
"1 & 0\n"
"\\end{pmatrix}\n"
"= -ZX.\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This implies that the following circuit is equivalent to the one just above up to a global phase factor of $-1$.\n"
msgstr "Ibig sabihin nito ay ang sumusunod na circuit ay katumbas ng nasa itaas hanggang sa global phase factor na $-1$.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![A ZX error for the 9-qubit Shor code](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/correcting-quantum-errors/Shor-ZX-error.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Isang ZX error para sa 9-qubit na Shor code](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/correcting-quantum-errors/Shor-ZX-error.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We can now move the $Z$ error just like before to obtain another equivalent circuit.\n"
msgstr "Maaari na nating ilipat ang $Z$ error tulad ng dati para makakuha ng isa pang katumbas na circuit.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![A ZX error for the 9-qubit Shor code](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/correcting-quantum-errors/Shor-ZX-error-2.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Isang ZX error para sa 9-qubit na Shor code](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/correcting-quantum-errors/Shor-ZX-error-2.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "At this point it's evident that if the procedure to detect and correct $X$ errors is performed first, the $X$ error will be corrected, after which the procedure for detecting and correcting $Z$ errors can be performed to eliminate the $Z$ error as before.\n"
msgstr "Sa puntong ito ay malinaw na kung ang pamamaraan para ma-detect at maitama ang mga $X$ error ay isasagawa muna, ang $X$ error ay maitatama, pagkatapos nito ay maaaring isagawa ang pamamaraan para ma-detect at maitama ang mga $Z$ error para alisin ang $Z$ error tulad ng dati.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Alternatively, the procedure to detect and correct $Z$ errors can be performed first.\n"
"The fact that this procedure works as expected, even in the presence of one or more $X$ errors, follows from the fact that $X$ gates on any of the nine qubits used for the encoding commute with all of the gates in our simplified circuit for measuring the syndrome for $Z$ errors.\n"
"Thus, this syndrome measurement will still correctly identify which block has been affected by a $Z$ error.\n"
"The fact that a $Z$ error on any block is corrected by applying a $Z$ gate to any qubit of that block, even if an $X$ error has also occurred, follows from the same argument as above concerning the ordering of $X$ and $Z$ gates giving us equivalent circuits up to a global phase.\n"
msgstr ""
"Bilang kahalili, maaaring isagawa muna ang pamamaraan para ma-detect at maitama ang mga $Z$ error.\n"
"Ang katotohanang gumagana ang pamamaraang ito ayon sa inaasahan, kahit na may isa o higit pang mga $X$ error, ay sumusunod sa katotohanang ang mga $X$ gate sa alinman sa siyam na qubit na ginamit para sa encoding ay nagko-commute sa lahat ng gate sa ating pinasimpleng circuit para sa pagsukat ng syndrome para sa mga $Z$ error.\n"
"Kaya, ang syndrome measurement na ito ay tama pa ring makakakilala kung aling bloke ang naapektuhan ng isang $Z$ error.\n"
"Ang katotohanang ang isang $Z$ error sa anumang bloke ay naitutuwid sa pamamagitan ng pag-apply ng isang $Z$ gate sa anumang qubit ng bloke na iyon, kahit na may $X$ error na nangyari rin, ay sumusunod sa parehong argumento sa itaas tungkol sa pagkakasunod-sunod ng $X$ at $Z$ gate na nagbibigay sa atin ng mga katumbas na circuit hanggang sa global phase.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"It follows that the 9-qubit Shor code can correct an $X$ error, a $Z$ error, or both, on any one of the nine qubits used for this code.\n"
"In fact, we can correct more errors than that, including multiple $X$ errors (as long as they fall into different blocks) or multiple $Z$ errors (as long as at most one block experiences an odd number of them) — but going forward, what will be most relevant for the purposes of this lesson is that we can correct an $X$ error, a $Z$ error, or both on any one qubit.\n"
msgstr ""
"Sumusunod na ang 9-qubit na Shor code ay maaaring magwasto ng isang $X$ error, isang $Z$ error, o pareho, sa alinman sa siyam na qubit na ginamit para sa code na ito.\n"
"Sa katunayan, maaari tayong magwasto ng higit pang mga error kaysa rito, kabilang ang maraming $X$ error (basta't nasa iba't ibang bloke ang mga ito) o maraming $Z$ error (basta't hanggang isang bloke lamang ang nakakaranas ng gansal na bilang ng mga ito) — ngunit sa pagsulong, ang pinaka-relevant para sa layunin ng araling ito ay maaari tayong magwasto ng isang $X$ error, isang $Z$ error, o pareho sa alinmang qubit.\n"

#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Error reduction for random errors {#error-reduction-for-random-errors}"
msgstr "Pagbabawas ng error para sa mga random na error {#error-reduction-for-random-errors}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Before we move on to the last section of the lesson, which concerns arbitrary quantum errors, let's briefly consider the performance of the 9-qubit Shor code when errors represented by Pauli matrices occur *randomly* on the qubits.\n"
msgstr "Bago tayo lumipat sa huling seksyon ng aralin, na tumutukoy sa mga arbitrary na quantum error, pag-usapan natin nang maikli ang performance ng 9-qubit na Shor code kapag ang mga error na kinakatawan ng mga Pauli matrix ay nagaganap nang *random* sa mga qubit.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To be more concrete, let's consider a simple noise model where errors occur *independently* on the qubits, with each qubit experiencing an error with probability $p$, and with no correlation between errors on different qubits\n"
"— along similar lines to a binary symmetric channel for classical bits.\n"
"We could assign different probabilities for $X$, $Y$, and $Z$ errors to occur, but to keep things as simple as possible, we'll consider the worst case scenario for the 9-qubit Shor code, which is that a $Y$ error occurs on each of the affected qubits.\n"
"A $Y$ error, by the way, is equivalent (up to an irrelevant global phase factor) to both an $X$ and a $Z$ error occurring on the same qubit, given that $Y = iXZ$.\n"
"This explains our apparent disregard of $Y$ errors up to this point.\n"
msgstr ""
"Para maging mas kongkreto, isaalang-alang natin ang isang simpleng noise model kung saan ang mga error ay nagaganap nang *nakapag-iisa* sa mga qubit, na ang bawat qubit ay nakakaranas ng error nang may probability na $p$, at walang kaugnayan sa pagitan ng mga error sa iba't ibang qubit\n"
"— katulad ng binary symmetric channel para sa mga classical bit.\n"
"Maaari tayong mag-assign ng iba't ibang probability para sa $X,$ $Y,$ at $Z$ error na magaganap, ngunit para panatilihing simple hangga't maaari, isasaalang-alang natin ang pinakamasahol na sitwasyon para sa 9-qubit na Shor code, na ang isang $Y$ error ang nagaganap sa bawat isa sa mga naapektuhang qubit.\n"
"Ang isang $Y$ error, sa pamamagitan ng paraan, ay katumbas (hanggang sa isang irrelevant na global phase factor) ng parehong $X$ at $Z$ error na nagaganap sa parehong qubit, dahil $Y = iXZ.$\n"
"Ipinapaliwanag nito ang ating tila pagwawalang-bahala sa mga $Y$ error hanggang sa puntong ito.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now, supposing that $\\mathsf{Q}$ is a qubit in some particular state that we'd like to protect against errors, we can consider the option to use the 9-qubit Shor code.\n"
"A natural question to ask is, \"Should we use it?\"\n"
msgstr ""
"Ngayon, ipagpalagay na ang $\\mathsf{Q}$ ay isang qubit sa ilang partikular na estado na nais nating protektahan laban sa mga error, maaari nating isaalang-alang ang opsyon na gamitin ang 9-qubit na Shor code.\n"
"Isang natural na tanong ang dapat itanong: \"Dapat ba nating gamitin ito?\"\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The answer is not necessarily \"yes.\"\n"
"If there's too much noise, meaning in this context that $p$ is too large, using the Shor code could actually make things worse — just like the 3-bit repetition code is worse than no code when $p$ is larger than one-half.\n"
"But, if $p$ is small enough, then the answer is \"yes,\" we should use the code, because it will decrease the likelihood that the encoded state becomes corrupted.\n"
"Let's see why this is, and what it means for $p$ to be too large or small enough for this code.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang sagot ay hindi kinakailangang \"oo.\"\n"
"Kung masyadong maraming ingay, ibig sabihin sa kontekstong ito na masyadong malaki ang $p$, ang paggamit ng Shor code ay maaaring talagang magpalala ng mga bagay — tulad ng 3-bit repetition code na mas masahol kaysa sa walang code kapag ang $p$ ay mas malaki sa isa't kalahati.\n"
"Ngunit, kung maliit lang ang $p$, ang sagot ay \"oo,\" dapat nating gamitin ang code, dahil babawasan nito ang posibilidad na masira ang naka-encode na estado.\n"
"Tingnan natin kung bakit ganito, at kung ano ang ibig sabihin para sa $p$ na masyadong malaki o maliit na sapat para sa code na ito.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The Shor code corrects any Pauli error on a single qubit, including a $Y$ error of course, but it doesn't properly correct two or more $Y$ errors.\n"
"To be clear, we're assuming that we're using the $X$ and $Z$ error corrections described earlier in the section.\n"
"(Of course, if we knew in advance that we only had to worry about $Y$ errors, we would naturally choose our corrections differently — but that's cheating the noise model, and we'd always be able to change the model by selecting different Pauli errors to make this new choice of corrections fail whenever two or more qubits are affected by errors.)\n"
msgstr ""
"Itinutuwid ng Shor code ang anumang Pauli error sa iisang qubit, kabilang ang isang $Y$ error siyempre, ngunit hindi nito naitutuwid nang wasto ang dalawa o higit pang mga $Y$ error.\n"
"Para maging malinaw, ipinapalagay natin na ginagamit natin ang mga $X$ at $Z$ error correction na inilarawan kanina sa seksyon.\n"
"(Siyempre, kung alam natin nang maaga na kailangan lamang nating mag-alala tungkol sa mga $Y$ error, natural na pipili tayo ng iba't ibang pagwawasto — ngunit iyan ay pandaraya sa noise model, at palagi tayong makakabago ng modelo sa pamamagitan ng pagpili ng iba't ibang Pauli error para gawing mabigo ang bagong pagpipiliang ito kapag naapektuhan ng mga error ang dalawa o higit pang qubit.)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "So, the code protects $\\mathsf{Q}$ so long as at most one of the nine qubits is affected by an error, which happens with probability\n"
msgstr "Kaya, pinoprotektahan ng code ang $\\mathsf{Q}$ basta't hanggang isang qubit lamang sa siyam ang naapektuhan ng error, na nangyayari nang may probability na\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"(1-p)^9 + 9 p (1-p)^8.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"(1-p)^9 + 9 p (1-p)^8.\n"
"$$\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Otherwise, with probability\n"
msgstr "Kung hindi, nang may probability na\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"1 - (1-p)^9 - 9 p (1-p)^8,\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"1 - (1-p)^9 - 9 p (1-p)^8,\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "the code fails to protect $\\mathsf{Q}$.\n"
msgstr "nabibigo ang code na protektahan ang $\\mathsf{Q}$.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Specifically, what that means in this context is that, up to a global phase, a non-identity Pauli operation will be applied to our qubit $\\mathsf{Q}$ (as a logical qubit).\n"
"That is, if $X$ and $Z$ errors are detected and corrected for the Shor code as described earlier in the lesson, we'll be left with the encoding of a state that's equivalent, up to a global phase, to the encoding of a non-identity Pauli operation applied to the original state of $\\mathsf{Q}$.\n"
"A more succinct way to say this is that a *logical* error will have occurred.\n"
"That may or may not have an effect on the original state of $\\mathsf{Q}$ — or in other words the *logical qubit* we've encoded with nine *physical qubits* — but, for the sake of this analysis, we're considering this event to mean failure.\n"
msgstr ""
"Sa partikular, ang ibig sabihin nito sa kontekstong ito ay, hanggang sa global phase, ang isang non-identity na Pauli operation ay ia-apply sa ating qubit na $\\mathsf{Q}$ (bilang isang logical qubit).\n"
"Ibig sabihin, kung ang mga $X$ at $Z$ error ay dide-detect at itatama para sa Shor code ayon sa inilarawan kanina sa aralin, matitirang may encoding ng isang estado na katumbas, hanggang sa global phase, ng encoding ng isang non-identity na Pauli operation na in-apply sa orihinal na estado ng $\\mathsf{Q}$.\n"
"Ang mas maikling paraan para sabihin ito ay ang isang *logical* error ang nagaganap.\n"
"Maaaring magkaroon o walang epekto iyon sa orihinal na estado ng $\\mathsf{Q}$ — o sa ibang salita ang *logical qubit* na ating na-encode gamit ang siyam na *physical qubit* — ngunit, para sa layunin ng pagsusuring ito, itinuturing natin ang pangyayaring ito bilang kabiguan.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"On the other hand, if we didn't bother to use the code, our one and only qubit would suffer a similar fate (of being subject to a non-identity Pauli operation) with probability $p$.\n"
"The code helps when the first probability is smaller than the second:\n"
msgstr ""
"Sa kabilang banda, kung hindi tayo nag-abala sa paggamit ng code, ang ating isa at tanging qubit ay maaaring makaranas ng katulad na kapalaran (ng pagiging subject sa isang non-identity na Pauli operation) nang may probability na $p$.\n"
"Nakakatulong ang code kapag ang unang probability ay mas maliit kaysa sa pangalawa:\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"1 - (1-p)^9 - 9 p (1-p)^8 < p.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"$$\n"
"1 - (1-p)^9 - 9 p (1-p)^8 < p.\n"
"$$\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Here's a plot that illustrates, for very small values of $p$, that the code provides an advantage, with the break-even point occurring at about $0.0323$.\n"
msgstr "Narito ang isang plot na naglalarawan, para sa napakaliit na mga halaga ng $p$, na nagbibigay ng kalamangan ang code, na ang break-even point ay nangyayari sa humigit-kumulang $0.0323$.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Error probability graph for independent Y errors using the Shor code](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/correcting-quantum-errors/breakeven-Shor.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Error probability graph para sa mga independent na Y error gamit ang Shor code](/learning/images/courses/foundations-of-quantum-error-correction/correcting-quantum-errors/breakeven-Shor.svg)\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"If $p$ is smaller than this break-even point, then the code helps;\n"
"at the break-even point the probabilities are equal, so we're just wasting our time along with 8 qubits if we use the code;\n"
"and beyond the break-even point we should absolutely not be using this code because it's *increasing* the chance of a logical error on $\\mathsf{Q}$.\n"
msgstr ""
"Kung ang $p$ ay mas maliit sa break-even point na ito, nakakatulong ang code;\n"
"sa break-even point ay pantay ang mga probability, kaya't nasasayang lang ang ating oras kasama ang 8 qubit kung gagamitin natin ang code;\n"
"at higit pa sa break-even point ay talagang hindi dapat gamitin ang code na ito dahil *dinadagdagan* nito ang posibilidad ng logical error sa $\\mathsf{Q}$.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Three and a quarter percent or so may not seem like a very good break-even point, particularly when compared to $50\\%$, which is the analogous break-even point for the 3-bit repetition code for classical information.\n"
"This difference is, in large part, due to the fact that quantum information is more delicate and harder to protect than classical information.\n"
"But also — while recognizing that the 9-qubit Shor code represents a brilliant discovery, as the world's first quantum error correcting code — it should be acknowledged that it isn't actually a very good code in practical terms.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang tatlo at isang-kapat na porsyento, humigit-kumulang, ay maaaring hindi mukhang napakagandang break-even point, lalo na kung ikukumpara sa $50\\%$, na siyang katumbas na break-even point para sa 3-bit repetition code para sa classical information.\n"
"Ang pagkakaibang ito ay, malaking bahagi nito, dahil sa katotohanang ang quantum information ay mas marupok at mas mahirap protektahan kaysa sa classical information.\n"
"Ngunit pati na rin — habang kinikilala na ang 9-qubit na Shor code ay kumakatawan sa isang magiting na natuklasan, bilang unang quantum error correcting code sa mundo — dapat pati na rin itong kilalanin na hindi ito talaga isang napakagandang code sa mga praktikal na termino.\n"
